WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.860
Good morning John, I am currently in Haiti.

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:06.950
No, I'm in my office, but by the time you
see this, I will be in Haiti. So I'm pre-recording

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:09.550
this video because I'm not gonna have internet
connection while I'm in Haiti.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:12.400
And people have been asking me to make a video
about the science of zombies, so I thought

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.070
that I would get that out of the way right
now.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:21.180
There is no science of zombies! There has
never been, nor will there ever be - an undead

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:22.180
creature.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:27.150
Life is an extraordinarily complicated balance
of inputs and outputs and temperatures and

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:33.180
reactants and wastes and fuels, and if any
of that stuff breaks down, none of it works.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.719
Homeostasis - look it up, and then stop worrying
about zombies.

00:00:37.719 --> 00:00:43.410
But this does lead me to an interesting thought:
How, in fiction, are supernatural beings created?

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:46.250
And what does that say about the people who
write those stories and the people who are

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:47.260
interested in those stories?

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:52.050
So zombies, for example, are generally created
by disease. Just like rabies, you get bit,

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.739
you get the disease, you get sick, you go
crazy and then you die, and then you un-die

00:00:55.739 --> 00:00:58.190
and then you, yourself, become a vector for
the disease.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:02.110
There are other examples for disease-based
conversion from natural to supernatural, uh,

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:05.799
werewolves being another one like zombies,
but also vampires.

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:09.450
Now vampires get their power from a disease,
but it seems very different from zombies,

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:14.409
and it is in fact, quite different from zombies
because zombies are a disease based in violence;

00:01:14.409 --> 00:01:20.599
whereas vampires are a disease based in romance-sexy-time.
Which kinda makes sense why women are more

00:01:20.599 --> 00:01:24.029
interested in the vampires stuff, and the
men are more interested in the zombie stuff,

00:01:24.029 --> 00:01:29.130
because it's like action movies versus chick-flicks.
Except that supernatural monsters version

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:30.259
of that argument.

00:01:30.259 --> 00:01:34.579
But all of this is pretty unsurprising because
sex, and disease and violence, probably some

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:38.909
of the most important forces in the lives
of early humans, but newer myths, created

00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:42.939
by fiction writers and a lot of comic writers
provide the same kind of insight.

00:01:42.939 --> 00:01:47.209
Superheroes nowadays don't so much get their
power from disease and sex and violence, superheroes,

00:01:47.209 --> 00:01:52.950
which began to be created in the 1930s or
so, get their power from things that we believe,

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:54.810
or believed, hold great power.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:58.159
Spiderman is a great example, because once
upon a time, he got his powers from being

00:01:58.159 --> 00:02:01.930
bitten by a radioactive spider, and that was
back when radioactivity and atomic power held

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:06.069
all this promise of potential, like, cheap
energy for everyone and also the potential

00:02:06.069 --> 00:02:08.039
for the destruction of the entire world.

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:12.310
But then when Spiderman got revamped for the
next generation, they cut out the radioactivity

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:15.860
- cause that wasn't sexy any more - and instead
they put in a genetically modified spider.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:20.830
And thus, we can say about ourselves as consumers
of content, that the aura of mystery and power

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:26.620
and fear that once surrounded radioactivity
and atomic energy now has moved over into

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:28.350
genetically modified stuff.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:32.569
All these things that within our culture hold
a great deal of power and mystique, like money

00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:36.069
and technical expertise and the government
as a hero and the government as a villain,

00:02:36.069 --> 00:02:41.780
atomic power, outer-space, the internet, particle
physics, our genes, our brains - our obsession

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:46.540
with all these things have grown and waned
over the years, as has their use in the creation

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:47.540
of superheroes.

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:51.459
And of course, John, in your zombie apocalypse
novella, you did this very thing - you took

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:56.060
a source of power and fear and mystery for
humanity, the genetic engineering of crops,

00:02:56.060 --> 00:03:00.030
and you mixed it with the existing zombie
myth to get zombies who died and then liked

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:03.629
to plant lots of corn. You fell directly into
the archetype, like everybody else. Of course,

00:03:03.629 --> 00:03:06.450
everyone falls in the archetype - that's what
archetypes are for.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:10.530
So I'm always keeping my eye out for new and
unique ways that superheroes are being created,

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:13.319
because I think it's a really clear indicator
of what we're becoming obsessed with and what

00:03:13.319 --> 00:03:14.319
we're becoming afraid of.

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:17.579
For example, there's a new movie coming out
in which an out of work writer takes this

00:03:17.579 --> 00:03:22.310
pill, and he becomes a super-genius, and of
course, this reflects our fear and fascination

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:25.400
about what is going on with brain chemistry
and pharmaceuticals.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:30.109
Superheroes and other super natural beings
are, of course, fictional, and they seem like

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:33.769
they're just there for fun, but they do provide
us, I think, with a great opportunity to analyze

00:03:33.769 --> 00:03:36.999
ourselves and what we're becoming obsessed
with and what we're becoming afraid of.

00:03:36.999 --> 00:03:40.290
Interestingly, those two things - often the
same thing.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:45.519
So it's nice to see that this entire shelf
of science fiction here, in fact, allows me

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:48.829
to learn more about us as humans.

00:03:48.829 --> 00:03:50.090
John, I'll see you on Monday.

